Apple HomeView is the company's new smart home display, and it is already on sale. This guide sticks to what Apple's own pages say: what the device does, what it needs from your phone, how its privacy design works, and which questions the company has left open. It ends with a plain view of who it suits and who might hold off.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited Apple pages for specs, prices and dates. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook has not used the device, and has no affiliate relationship with Apple or any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated and is not official Apple artwork.*

## Name, price and date: what Apple's pages show

Apple's own pages give the name, price and timing. Its newsroom lists HomeView at $299 in the US, and the wall-mounted version costs the same. Apple says pre-orders began on 13 October 2026 in Australia, Canada, India, New Zealand, the UK and the US, with sales starting 13 November. Singapore pre-orders opened on 15 October, and the October newsroom says South Africa is served through Apple Authorized Resellers. Apple says it is not for sale in the European Union.

One small difference: the specs page and store page do not themselves give a date or delivery estimate. The 13 November date comes from Apple's newsroom posts.

## What it is

Apple describes a 7-inch Liquid Retina display on a domed speaker base, joined by a tilting hinge. There is also a wall-mounted model with the same display. The specs page lists an A18 chip, a 12MP Center Stage camera, Wi-Fi 6E, Bluetooth 6.0 and a USB-C connector for power. The tabletop model needs wired power and ships with a cable and a 30W adapter. The tabletop model weighs 2.1 lb (Apple notes weight varies by manufacturing process).

Colours differ by model. The tabletop comes in silver, starlight, rose pink and space gray. The wall-mounted model comes in silver and space gray. Apple's newsroom lists Schneider Home power mounts for the wall-mounted model, sold separately from $59.95, with a PoE version due in early 2027. The wall-mounted model ships without a power adapter, and Apple says 20W or more is required.

## What it does

**Apple says:**

- **Personal profiles.** It can recognise up to six people by face, and shows each person their own Messages, FaceTime, Calendar, Reminders and Find My content.
- **Entertainment and calls.** Apple Music, Podcasts, Apple TV, FaceTime, Intercom and multiroom audio are listed.
- **Cooking.** Recipe steps, hands-free timers and ingredient tracking.
- **Home control.** It controls HomeKit and Matter accessories by touch or voice, and can show up to nine camera feeds.
- **Siri AI.** Apple calls it a new version of Siri with broad world knowledge, conversation and onscreen awareness.

Apple's own wording on the Siri limits matters. Siri AI on HomeView is in beta in software version 27.2, and some server-side features have daily usage limits. Apple says expanded access will be available for a fee in the future. It is offered in English in eight countries, and Apple says it is not available in the EU.

## Matter, Thread and the home hub role

Apple's support page says HomeView becomes a home hub automatically during setup. A hub lets you control accessories remotely, share access and run automations, and you need one to add Matter accessories to the Home app. Only the home owner can add it to the Home app.

On Thread, the specs page lists Thread networking and says it works with HomeKit- or Matter-enabled Thread accessories. However, the support page that explains Thread border routers names HomePod mini and Apple TV 4K as examples and does not name HomeView. Whether HomeView can serve as a border router for your Thread devices is therefore something to confirm with Apple before relying on it.

## Camera and privacy

Apple says camera and microphone data for recognition is processed in Secure Exclaves on the A18 chip, which it says HomeView software, apps, users and Apple cannot access. Its support page says that data is "never recorded or stored", and that HomeView keeps no video or audio feed of your home.

Personal content only appears when three things are true. The device recognises your face, your paired iPhone or iPad is on the same Wi-Fi, and that phone was recently unlocked. Apple's face-recognition support page says personal content is stored on the paired iPhone or iPad, and that HomeView keeps only the limited personal data it needs for features like Siri and recognition. Face and voice profiles are end-to-end encrypted in iCloud. Guests see only guest-accessible features such as timers, weather, music and accessory controls.

## What you need

Apple says setup needs an iPhone on iOS 27.2 or later, or an iPad on iPadOS 27.2 or later. Each household member needs an Apple Account and must be added in the Home app. For Siri AI to use personal context, you need a paired Apple Intelligence-enabled iPhone or iPad. Without one, Apple says you still get world knowledge, conversation and music discovery. Accessories, Apple Music and some apps are sold or subscribed separately.

## What Apple has not said

![Two-column chart separating what Apple confirms about HomeView from what its pages leave unclear](/images/site1/apple-homeview-explained-who-its-for-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited Apple pages.*

- Whether HomeView works as a Thread border router, as noted above.
- Any price outside the US was not checked for this post, so treat $299 as the US figure.
- How much the paid tier of server-side Apple Intelligence will cost, or what the daily limits are.
- A delivery estimate on the store page. Shipping times were not shown when we looked.
- Storage and memory figures, which the specs page does not list.

## Who it is for, and who should wait

**It looks like a good fit if** your household already uses iPhones, the Home app and Apple Music, and you want a shared screen in the kitchen or hallway that shows each person their own calendar. The face-based profiles are the main idea, so homes with several Apple users get the most from it.

**Consider waiting if:**

- You use Android or have no iPhone or iPad, since setup and personal features depend on one.
- You want Thread accessories to rely on it as a border router, until Apple clarifies.
- You want Siri AI finished. It is labelled beta, with usage limits and a future fee mentioned.
- You live in the EU, where it is not for sale.
- You plan to use Home Collection cameras, locks or thermostats. Apple says these arrive starting in December.

## Comparison with Echo Show and Nest Hub

This post does not compare numbers with Amazon's Echo Show or Google's Nest Hub. Those specs were not checked on the makers' own pages for this article, so any side-by-side would be guesswork. Check the current official pages for those products before deciding.

## The takeaway

Apple has confirmed the key facts: a $299 US price, a 13 November sale date and a 7-inch screen that doubles as a home hub. It has left a few practical questions open, including Thread behaviour and the cost of future AI access. If you live in the Apple ecosystem and the supported regions, it is worth a look. If your needs depend on the unanswered items, wait for clearer answers.
